    "q-98116": {
      "dom_id": "step-q98116",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=1$ and $f(b)=6,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c?$",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ 6-1 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨2,3⟩-⟨1,2⟩) \\\\ 5 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 5 & ={f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
