    "q-130895": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130895",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=x-{y}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨-1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨1,4⟩,$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=x-{y}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨-1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨1,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,4)-f(-1,2) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,4⟩-⟨-1,2⟩) \\\\ (1-{4}^{2})-(-1-{2}^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1-(-1),4-2⟩ \\\\ -15-(-5) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,2⟩ \\\\ -10 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨1,-2y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨1,-2{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,2⟩ \\\\ -10 & =⟨1,-2{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,2⟩ \\\\ -10 & =2-4{c}_{2} \\\\ -12 & =-4{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{4-2} & =\\frac{x-(-1)}{1-(-1)} \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{2} & =\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y & =x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={c}_{1}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{2}=3,$ we obtain that ${c}_{1}=3-3=0.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(0,3).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
