    "q-130903": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130903",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Determine the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-xy+1$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3⟩,$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-xy+1$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,3)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,3⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ -1-1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ -2 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨3{x}^{2}-y,-x⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2},-{c}_{1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ -2 & =⟨3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2},-{c}_{1}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ -2 & =3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{3-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{1-0} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2} & =x \\\\ y & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}=-2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-(2{c}_{1}+1)-2{c}_{1} & =-2 \\\\ 3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-4{c}_{1}+1 & =0 \\\\ (3{c}_{1}-1)({c}_{1}-1) & =0 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=2(\\frac{1}{3})+1=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the first point are $(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{5}{3}).$\n\n- For ${c}_{1}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=2(1)+1=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the second point are $(1,3).$\n\nFinally we can conclude that the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem are $(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{5}{3})$ and $(1,3).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-130903-a",
