    "q-36808": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36808",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+yz$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨1,2,3⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3,4⟩,$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+yz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨1,2,3⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3,4)-f(1,2,3) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3,4⟩-⟨1,2,3⟩) \\\\ 20-8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4x,z,y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{1},{c}_{3},{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 12 & =⟨4{c}_{1},{c}_{3},{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 12 & =4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{1}=\\frac{z-3}{1} \\\\ x-1 & =y-2=z-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,y=t+2,\\,z=t+3,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t+1,\\,{c}_{2}=t+2,\\,{c}_{3}=t+3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $12=4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(t+1)+(t+2)+(t+3) & =12 \\\\ 4t+4+t+2+t+3 & =12 \\\\ 6t+9 & =12 \\\\ 6t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}+2=\\frac{5}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{7}{2}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
