    "q-106860": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106860",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,5⟩,$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,5⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4,5)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4,5⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 40-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =⟨{c}_{2}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =2{c}_{2}{c}_{3}+4{c}_{1}{c}_{3}+5{c}_{1}{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{4}=\\frac{z-0}{5} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{y}{4}=\\frac{z}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,\\,y=4t,\\,z=5t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=4t,\\,{c}_{3}=5t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{2}{c}_{3}+4{c}_{1}{c}_{3}+5{c}_{1}{c}_{2}=40,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4t)(5t)+4(2t)(5t)+5(2t)(4t) & =40 \\\\ 40{t}^{2}+40{t}^{2}+40{t}^{2} & =40 \\\\ 120{t}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}<0<1,$ it's clear that $t=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ corresponds to a point that does not lie on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we can ignore it. Thus, $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nFor $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},$ we can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$  This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =5(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-106860-a",
